# PROJECT_NAME — agent context

> Generated by `orch init --template data-pipeline` (GENERATED_AT).

## About orch (this project is orch-managed)

- `orch` walks `tasks.json` as a DAG and dispatches each task to
  Claude / Codex / OpenCode / Gemini in parallel.
- **CLI missing?** `pipx install orch`
  (repo: <https://github.com/hectorcanaimero/orch>).
- **Never edit `tasks.json` status directly** — runtime state lives in
  SQLite. Use `orch task set --id ID --status STATUS` or one of the
  `scripts/task-*.sh` helpers.
- **Full agent context on demand**: if you use Claude Code, run
  `orch install-skills` once — the `/orch` skill loads only when the
  agent needs it (token-cheap). Otherwise `orch --help` is the source
  of truth.

## Stack

- Python `>=3.11`
- **DuckDB** `>=1.1` — the warehouse (single file at `warehouse/main.duckdb`)
- `pandas` `>=2.2` — extract-side DataFrames
- `httpx` `>=0.27` — HTTP client with timeouts and retries
- `apscheduler` `>=3.10` — cron-style scheduling in-process
- Dev: `pytest`, `ruff`

## Layout

- `pipeline/`
  - `extract.py` — one function per source type (HTTP, CSV, S3)
  - `transform.py` — runs every `.sql` under `transforms/` in filename order
  - `load.py` — idempotent append into `raw.<table>`
  - `jobs.py` — APScheduler entry point, `python -m pipeline.jobs`
- `sources.yaml` — per-source config (url, headers, format, cron, natural key)
- `transforms/` — hand-written SQL that reads from `raw.*` and writes to `marts.*`
- `warehouse/` — DuckDB file (gitignored)
- `.orchestrator/` — orch state (config, model router, SQLite)

## Conventions

- **Load is idempotent.** Every extract can re-run without duplicating rows — dedupe on the natural key declared in `sources.yaml`. Never rely on "the scheduler won't fire twice."
- **Transforms are pure SQL over DuckDB.** No pandas in `transforms/*.sql`. Keep the mart layer readable by a data analyst who doesn't know Python.
- **Schema-per-layer**: `raw.*` for extractor output (append-only), `marts.*` for business-ready aggregates. No cross-layer selects out of order.
- **Timeouts everywhere.** `httpx.get(..., timeout=30)`. A hung source must not stall the scheduler.
- **Every extractor ships with a test in the same PR** using an in-memory DuckDB (`duckdb.connect(":memory:")`) — fast, no I/O.

## Orch state

- SQLite at `.orchestrator/state/PROJECT_NAME/orch.db`.
- `tasks_definition` (static): id, model, backend, deps, phase, estimate.
- `tasks_runtime` (dynamic): status, attempts, last_model, last_backend.
- **Never edit runtime status in `tasks.json`** — use `orch task set --id ID --status STATUS` instead.

## Useful commands

```bash
python -m pipeline.jobs                   # start the scheduler
python -c "import duckdb; con = duckdb.connect('warehouse/main.duckdb'); print(con.sql('SHOW TABLES').fetchall())"
pytest -q                                 # run the suite
orch status                               # human table
orch run --dry-run                        # preview the plan
orch dashboard --profile stakeholder      # share the URL with your client
```
